> Which one would be better?

We did spec it, in "Re: Supporting status trailers in emails" [1].
You chose Superseded-by, as a body trailer at the end of a reply
rather than a mail header, and said the latest trailer wins. I still
think that is the better one, for three reasons:

- It goes in the old thread. Whoever opens a stale thread finds the
  pointer to its replacement right there, which is the link you said
  matters.
- It can be written after the fact, by anyone, by replying to the old
  series. Supersedes has to be written from the new series, so it
  cannot retire something already posted without new mail in the
  wrong thread.
- Supersedes would duplicate the "Link to vN" line, which already
  marks the old version from the new side.

> Let's just get the logic now, then we'll follow up with a test of a few
> threads.

The logic is posted as "[PATCH v3] notmuch: Tag threads a
Superseded-by trailer names" [2]. It is the trailer patch from the
v2 series, which I dropped from "[PATCH v3] notmuch: Tag series a
later version replaces" [3] to keep that one simple. It needs [3],
so apply that one first.

To retire a thread, reply to any message in it and end the reply with

    Superseded-by: id:<message-id of the replacement>

as the last paragraph, or just above a signature. On the next
"notmuch new", the whole thread is tagged superseded. A quoted
trailer, or one with more text below it, does not count.

On a copy of the database, it tagged the threads of the test replies
I wrote and nothing else on the list. For your test, the seven series
no Link line catches are listed in "Re: Supporting status trailers in
emails" [4]. Any of them will do. Remember to run "just install" in
dotfiles/notmuch first, since notmuch runs the installed hook.

